{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_ehps66somvvwp42ujugujnvospwfoj56wqpbl4punyyvu2vp2raa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Rust translation of the C base122 library compiles cleanly in both dev and release modes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI translated the C base122 encoder/decoder to safe, idiomatic Rust in `src/base122.rs`. The implementation:\n\n- **`Base122Error`** — wraps a `String` message and implements `Display` and `Error`.\n- **`BitReader`** — reads up to 8 bits at a time, mirroring the C `bitreader_read` logic by combining two bytes into a `u16`, shifting, and masking. Returns `(bits_read, value)`.\n- **`BitWriter`** — writes up to 8 bits at a time, supporting a count-only mode (when `output` is `None`) for the size-determination pass. Uses the same bit-mask/shift sequence the C `bitwriter_write` uses.\n- **`is_illegal` / `get_illegal_index`** — direct mirror of the C lookups against the 6 illegal byte values (`0, 10, 13, 34, 38, 92`).\n- **`encode` / `encode_internal`** — runs the encoder twice (count pass with `None`, then write pass) and reproduces the C two-byte expansion logic for illegal/incomplete chunks (including the `0x7 << 2` shortened-tail case).\n- **`decode` / `decode_internal` / `write_last_7`** — handles single-byte sequences, two-byte sequences (`110xxx1y` / `10xxxxxx`), the shortened-tail case (`illegalIndex == 0x7`), and produces the same error messages the C version uses so behavioural tests match.\n\n`cargo build` and `cargo build --release` both succeed with no warnings or `unimplemented!()`/`todo!()`/`panic!()` placeholders, and the translation contains no `unsafe`, no FFI, and no `libc` usage."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":209,"cache_read_input_tokens":43454,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":209,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standar